ATTRIBUTION AND THIRD-PARTY NOTICES
===================================

This project (hierarchical-lab) is a study project and reimplementation that is
inspired by, and derived in part from, the following work:

    Deep_Hierarchical_Classification
    https://github.com/Ugenteraan/Deep_Hierarchical_Classification
    Copyright (c) 2021 Ugenteraan Manogaran
    Licensed under the MIT License.

Specifically, the following portions of this project follow that project's
approach and should be considered derived from it:

  * models.py — the coarse-to-fine classifier head, in which the coarse (20-way)
    logits are concatenated into the fine (100-way) head. This mirrors the
    structure of `model/resnet50.py` in the original project.

  * losses.py — the layer loss (`layer_loss`) and the dependency loss
    (`faithful_dloss`). The dependency loss is reproduced faithfully from
    `model/hierarchical_loss.py` for the purpose of analysis and discussion.

  * hierarchy.py — the CIFAR-100 superclass/subclass taxonomy, equivalent in
    content to `level_dict.py` in the original project.

The MIT License terms of the original work continue to apply to those portions.
The full text of the MIT License is reproduced in the LICENSE file of this
repository and applies equally to the original work.

The original project is itself an unofficial implementation of the paper:

    "Deep Hierarchical Classification for Category Prediction in E-commerce System"
    Wan, Zhang, Liu, Zhao, Xu, Wang (2020)
    https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.06692

Where this repository critiques aspects of the original implementation, those
critiques are offered as good-faith technical observations about specific
implementation details, not as criticism of the project or its author. The
original being public and readable is what made this study possible.


DATASET
-------

CIFAR-100 is used for the experiments in this repository. It is not distributed
with this project; it is downloaded at runtime.

    Learning Multiple Layers of Features from Tiny Images
    Alex Krizhevsky, 2009
    https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~kriz/cifar.html
